{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "So Much to Do at Work", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Who Will Be the Lucky One?", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 11, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 12, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B2.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": " Discuss factual and abstract information about common everyday or job related topics in both work and social contexts appropriate for the interlocutor.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": " Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": " Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": " Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "capable", "type": "name"}, {"content": "having the qualities or ability needed to do something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I don't think I'm capable of keeping up with the position.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob11aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are the words to review.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "book jacket ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a paper cover of a book which you can remove", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  We'll have been writing copy for book jackets for ages.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob11aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to copy", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to look at someone else's work and write the same thing as they have", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I copied your notes which I still owe you for. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob11aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "legwork", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the hard tedious work that has to be done in order to achieve something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: You can thank me by doing all the legwork for our next assignment.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob11aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to get a hold of", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to contact", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example:  I've been trying to get a hold of this person I want to interview. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob11aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le12ob11re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Passive Infinitives and Gerunds", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the passive infinitive or -ing form in some constructions using the<span class=\"strong\"> passive voice</span>. <br/><br/>  You use the<span class=\"strong\"> passive voice </span>when you want to focus on the receiver of the action, rather than the doer (agent).", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "If the subject is not the agent, you use a <span class=\"underline\">passive infinitive</span>:<br/><br/>  For example:<br/><br/>  <span class=\"italic\">The systems are <span class=\"strong\">to be checked</span> as soon as possible. </span><br/><br/>  You use <span class=\"underline\">passive -ing forms</span> after verbs that are normally followed by a gerund (love, recall etc.)<br/><br/>  For example:<br/><br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">love being given</span> chocolates!</span><br/><br/>  You can use the passive -ing form with the meaning of <span class=\"italic\">because</span>. <br/><br/>  For example:<br/><br/>  <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Having been bitten</span> by a dog, I have no love of them.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\"> Having been given</span> all these tasks as an editorial assistant has kept me busy.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There'll be a lot <span class=\"strong\"> to be taken on</span>. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Besides, I think<span class=\"strong\">  being promoted </span>to junior editor would be cool.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " The Future Continuous and Future Perfect Tenses", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the <span class=\"strong\"> Future Continuous</span> to say that something will be in progress at a particular time in the future or is expected to happen in the future.<br/><br/>  You can use the <span class=\"strong\">Future Perfect Continuous</span> tense to say that something will being happening by a certain time in the future.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Normally, you do not use<span class=\"underline\"> stative verbs</span> in the continuous tense because they describe states rather than actions. Some examples of <span class=\"underline\">stative verbs </span>are: <span class=\"italic\">love, doubt, care, believe, remember</span>.<br/> <br/>There are some <span class=\"underline\">stative verbs</span> you can use in the<span class=\"strong\"> Future Continuous</span> tense: <br/><br/>  1. When they have an active meaning<br/> <span class=\"italic\">I<span class=\"strong\">'ll be tasting</span> it to see if it is good or not.</span><br/><br/>  2. When they emphasize change or development<br/> <span class=\"italic\">More schools <span class=\"strong\">will be including</span> exercise classes as part of their curriculum.</span><br/><br/>   The structure of the<span class=\"strong\">  Future Perfect Continuous</span> tense is:<br/><br/>  subject + will have been + verb-ing <br/><br/>  For example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">We <span class=\"strong\">will</span> only <span class=\"strong\">have been working</span> here for a month</span>. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "We<span class=\"strong\">'ll have been working</span> in the industry for a while.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We<span class=\"strong\">'ll have been writing</span> copy for book jackets for ages.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I<span class=\"strong\">'ll be applying</span> for the position.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "He<span class=\"strong\">'ll be thinking about </span>what he can do.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Think about the expression \u201cto bite off more than you can chew\u201d. Write about a time when you have done this. When did you realize it was too much for you to do by yourself?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "After you have written down your response, think of the advice you would give someone who was in a similar situation.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use modals, passive infinitives and gerunds when giving advice. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I identify what an expression means and make a connection to my life.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write about an event in my personal or work life.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use my personal experience to give someone else advice.", "type": "text"}]}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "img_path": "", "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can discuss hopes and expectations for the future.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can express necessity, duty, and advice.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can express my work responsibilities.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "  Do you enjoy looking for and applying to jobs? Why or why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " How do you decide if you want you to apply for a job? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": " Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": " Let's review!